/* A designed loss (wave 6), composed onto ScreenScaffold. bible 4.11 makes
 * this a TEACHING screen, not a failure screen: the diagnosis is the content
 * and the Pale grant is the recovery.
 *
 * PHASE 9 TASK 18 GAVE IT THE HANDOFF'S BAND, WHICH IS THE ONLY STRUCTURAL
 * CHANGE. `Wave Defeat.dc.html:31` is a `HeroBand` in coral - the same
 * `linear-gradient(170deg, ...)` at `--radius-band` with the `.elev-2` pair
 * that six violet screens already draw - and `HeroBand.Tint` is the hook that
 * made it reachable. The kicker, the verdict, the sentence and the three cells
 * are the band's own column (`:40-56`); the card under it is the handoff's
 * teaching row (`:88-93`) rather than its `Defender performance` chart, which
 * needs per-creature damage this project's `WaveReport` does not carry.
 *
 * THIRTY-ONE DECLARATIONS BECAME TEN IN PHASE 8 TASK 11 AND NONE OF THE
 * TWENTY-ONE WAS REPLACED BY A COPY HERE, which is the test of whether a
 * screen composes the layer or re-derives it. That still holds of everything
 * below: the ramp, the radius and the elevation are `HeroBand`'s, the card is
 * `SectionCard`'s, the cells are `StatCell`'s, the chip is `TraitChip`'s, and
 * what is left is the geometry the handoff gives THIS screen and nothing else.
 *   - the root's `padding: var(--gutter)` and `align-items: center` are the
 *     scaffold's content region, which puts the gutter on
 *     `unity-scroll-view__content-container` so an elevated card's shadow is
 *     not clipped at the viewport edge.
 *   - `__verdict`'s size, weight and ink are `.t-hero` and `.t-danger`. Both
 *     are single-class selectors in Theme.uss and `.t-danger` is declared
 *     after `.t-hero`, so the coral wins the colour and the 28px survives.
 *   - `__diagnosis`'s --surface fill, --radius-card, 1px --divider border and
 *     --space-4 of padding were SectionCard's surface written out again,
 *     minus the elevation. Its --text-card-title is `.t-card-title`.
 *   - `__granted`'s size, weight and ink are `.t-secondary` and `.t-success`.
 *   - `__grants`'s --green-tint fill, row radius, 12px padding and
 *     `width: 100%` are gone and nothing took their place: that panel drew a
 *     full-width green block behind a single 160px card, and the whole block
 *     drew blank on a defeat with no resupply. WaveDefeatView.Bind has it.
 *   - `__retry`'s top margin and full width are the CTA row's own padding and
 *     its `flex-direction: column`, and the button's whole treatment is
 *     Theme's `.btn-primary`.
 */
.wave-defeat-view { flex-grow: 1; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the band */

/* `:31` - `margin: 10px 12px 0`. The 12 is the scaffold's gutter and is not
 * restated; the 10 is measured to the band's own SURFACE, and `.hero-band` is
 * the `.elev-2` wrapper, which already carries --elev-2-spread (12px) of
 * padding above it - that is where the drop is drawn. 12 is already more than
 * 10, so nothing is added here and the residual is 2px the shadow is using.
 *
 * THE BOTTOM IS THE ONE THAT NEEDED SAYING. `:60` puts the card 11px under the
 * band; the wrapper pays 12 below the surface and `.section-card` pays another
 * 6 above its own, so without this the gap is 18. -7 brings it to 11 exactly.
 * A LITERAL because USS on 6000.6.0f1 has no `calc()` - the same reason
 * `.elev-1`'s own negative margins are literals. */
.wave-defeat-view__band { margin-bottom: -7px; }

/* THE BAND IS A PADDED COLUMN, NOT A CENTRED SUBJECT, which is the shape two
 * of the six violet instances already take (`Splice Chamber:101`,
 * `Hybrid Growth:48`) and which `HeroBand.uss`'s own note names as the
 * direction a screen's sheet is allowed to reach - downward into its
 * descendants, never upward. `:31`'s `padding: 16px 18px 18px`. */
/* `align-items: stretch` AND NOT `flex-start`, WHICH IS A DEFECT THIS SCREEN
 * SHIPPED INTO A CAPTURE AND THE CAPTURE IS WHAT CAUGHT IT. `HeroBand`'s
 * subject is `align-items: center` by default, and the obvious correction for
 * a padded column is `flex-start` - which shrink-wraps every child to its own
 * content, so the three `StatCell`s (`flex-basis: 0`, sized by the row) came
 * out as three overlapping 12px slivers with their labels running through each
 * other. `PostWaveView.uss` records the identical failure from the other
 * direction ("centring the content container makes every sibling shrink-wrap,
 * which collapses the stat row into overlapping cells"), which is how the
 * defect was recognised rather than re-debugged. `stretch` is flexbox's own
 * default and is what the handoff's block-level column has. */
.wave-defeat-view__band .hero-band__subject {
    align-items: stretch;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    padding-top: 16px;
    padding-bottom: 18px;
    padding-left: 18px;
    padding-right: 18px;
}

/* `:40` - the kicker, in the handoff's own coral (`#c07f74`; --coral-text is
 * #b3564a, 17 away in summed channel distance and the project's one
 * coral-on-light ink). `.t-danger` after `.t-micro` carries the colour.
 *
 * THE HEIGHT IS 16b's RULE ON A SIXTH INSTANCE, AND THE CAPTURE IS WHAT
 * MEASURED IT. Scanned down the band's ink rows on `WaveDefeatView.png` at the
 * 430 frame: the band's surface opens at y=12 and its `padding-top: 16px`
 * puts the first content at y=28, but the kicker's glyphs are at y=39..46 -
 * eleven pixels lower, which is one whole --text-micro line box (10 x 1.36 =
 * 13.6, `Nunito-Bold SDF.asset:149,152`) of leading that nothing declares.
 * Every Label that is a CHILD of this column pays it once: the verdict below,
 * the sentence under that, and `StatCell`'s two. 14 is that line box rounded
 * up, and `middle-left` puts the glyphs back on it. */
.wave-defeat-view__kicker {
    height: 14px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* `:41` - 34px against `.t-hero`'s 28. The handoff's own size is six over the
 * top of this project's type scale and --text-hero is the step below it; a
 * ninth display size invented for one screen is the drift the scale exists to
 * stop. Recorded as the residual rather than argued with silently - the same
 * trade `StatCell.uss` records for its value's 11 against 12.
 *
 * `letter-spacing: -.02em` AT 28px IS -0.56px, and UI Toolkit's
 * `letter-spacing` is in POINTS with no em unit. Rounded to 0 rather than
 * spent: half a pixel of negative tracking on a two-word headline is under the
 * rasteriser's own resolution at this size. */
/* NO `white-space: normal` BESIDE THE HEIGHT, which fix round 1 is right to
 * call a latent clip: a Label that is allowed to wrap inside a box pinned to
 * ONE line box loses every line after the first, silently. `Verdict` is a
 * two-word const and cannot wrap today, and the rule three below explains why
 * the sentence UNDER it - which really does wrap - gets no height at all. The
 * two declarations are mutually exclusive and are kept that way by
 * construction rather than by the constant's current length. */
.wave-defeat-view__verdict {
    height: 38px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    margin-top: 2px;
}

/* `:42` - the sentence under the verdict, 12.5px against `.t-card-title`'s 13,
 * wrapping inside `max-width: 290px`. The max-width is dropped rather than
 * translated: the band's own padding leaves 370 at the 406 content width, and
 * pinning a paragraph to 290 in a frame whose width is not the handoff's is a
 * line break in a fixed place rather than a measure. `white-space: normal` is
 * what makes it a paragraph at all - this is the longest sentence on the
 * screen with the wave-6 breach in it. */
/* NO HEIGHT HERE, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS SCREEN CANNOT APPLY 16b's
 * RULE. The two Labels above are single lines with a known line box; this one
 * WRAPS - `WaveDefeatScreen.Headline` runs to one line for "Lash broke
 * through." and two for the wave-6 sentence - so a pinned height would either
 * clip the long case or pad the short one. It pays the same one extra line box
 * the kicker's note measures (~18px at --text-card-title), and that is the
 * named residual on this screen. */
.wave-defeat-view__headline {
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: 5px;
}

/* `:43` - `display: flex; gap: 7px; margin-top: 13px`.
 *
 * THE -4px IS `StatCell.uss`'s OWN PRESCRIPTION AND NOT A NUDGE: a cell
 * carries half the handoff's 8px gap on each of its own sides, and that file
 * says what a caller that wants the row flush does about it. Without it the
 * row is inset 4px from the band's padding on both sides. The residual is the
 * inter-cell gap: 4+4 = 8 against the handoff's 7, one pixel over, twice. */
.wave-defeat-view__stats {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 13px;
    margin-left: -4px;
    margin-right: -4px;
}

/* `:44` - `background: rgba(255,255,255,.72)` over the coral ramp. Composited
 * against `#f6e2e4` that is (252, 247, 247), which is --surface to 7 in summed
 * channel distance, so the token is the colour rather than an approximation of
 * it - and USS here has no way to write a translucent white that is not a raw
 * rgba(). `StatCell`'s own --surface-sunk (#f8f6fc) is a violet-white and
 * reads cold on a coral band, which is the reason this rule exists at all.
 *
 * `:46` - the value in `#8e3f33`. --coral-text (#b3564a) is the project's
 * coral-on-light ink, 74 away in summed channel distance and the only one
 * there is; the alternative is a ninth coral invented for three numerals.
 * The LABEL is left on `t-micro`'s --mute rather than tinted to the handoff's
 * `#c07f74`, because a cell's caption is the component's and every other stat
 * row in the app reads the same grey. Both are named in the report. */
.wave-defeat-view__stats > .stat-cell { background-color: var(--surface); }
.wave-defeat-view__stats > .stat-cell .stat-cell__value { color: var(--coral-text); }

/* ------------------------------------------------------- the teaching card */

/* The handoff's two-sided card header (`:61`) - an uppercase eyebrow on the
 * left and something on the right. `SectionCard`'s own heading is one 19px
 * Baloo line and cannot be both, which is why the card is built without one;
 * `DeployView.CardHeader` and `SpliceChamberView.CardHeader` are the same row
 * on the same reasoning, and this is the fourth screen in the phase to want
 * it - which is the point at which it should become a component. Recorded.
 *
 * `align-items: center` RATHER THAN THE HANDOFF'S `baseline`, which UI Toolkit
 * does not have at all. */
.wave-defeat-view__diagnosis-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

.wave-defeat-view__diagnosis {
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the resupply */

.wave-defeat-view__resupply { flex-direction: column; }

/* Sits directly over the cards it introduces, so it reads as their caption
 * rather than as a line of its own. */
.wave-defeat-view__granted { margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }

/* The resupply, laid out like RosterView's grid and for the same reason: a
 * CreatureCard is a fixed 160px, two of them plus the 12px gutter leave 406
 * of space for 320 of card, and `space-between` is what puts a pair against
 * both edges and an odd third at the left where the next row starts. */
.wave-defeat-view__grants {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

.wave-defeat-view__grant { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
